 [17592186050682 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050682 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050682 #edb/ref 1031 "Independence of Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050682 #edb/ref 1033 3863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050682 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5e9ef58b-352e-5161-ad27-7cfe61e49ba0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050738 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050738 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186172070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050738 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186172071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050738 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186172072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050738 #edb/ref 1031 "Linear Combinations of Binomial Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050738 #edb/ref 1033 3295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050738 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "627c885e-7b99-5dc6-9c1e-29459e6cad26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1031 "Independence of Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1033 3048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050835 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051022 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051022 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051022 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051022 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051022 #edb/ref 1031 "The Trinomial Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051022 #edb/ref 1033 2989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051022 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7af6aaf0-d8dd-5ee1-8ae9-6b5b69906740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161713 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161713 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161713 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1fdcc887-4c52-5d91-ab28-63a7dce9fe5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161713 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.28.$ Likewise, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.4 \\cdot 0.7 = 0.28. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.12=0.4\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.42=0.6\\cdot 0.7={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.18=0.6\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161714 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161714 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,2).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_Y(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.55$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161714 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e12d6b6-5771-5e54-8f16-7899f82d90bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161714 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161715 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161715 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.24$ | $0.06$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.56$ | $0.14$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_Y(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161715 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1875d098-95f6-50a7-b069-56865207b3dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161715 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.24.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.3 \\cdot 0.8 = 0.24. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =0.56=0.7\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.06=0.3\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.14=0.7\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162153 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Joint Probabilities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162154 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162155 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1045 "Independence of Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162186 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4373edc4-1d40-5e9a-bfe9-ecc0845c2651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162343 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162343 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162343 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25122d3d-9cb1-5f5e-b327-d0566bb4de5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162343 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162343 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.75\\cdot 0.8=0.6.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.05=0.25\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.15=0.75\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.20=0.25\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162457 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162457 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162457 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162457 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162457 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b10e877-0140-5f79-97b2-25494c500279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162457 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162457 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.8=0.56.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1061 44847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b566ab50-5b2d-5693-aaa2-b74aeb985d53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1061 44848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85e8d07f-ff5e-5a08-b15e-b9682157622a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1061 44851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bf9c7bd-4281-5c38-aa27-271d95010465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1061 44854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0b7073a-7020-52ef-b9e8-f169d03cd0f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163365 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ respectively. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(X=x, Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively, then\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y.$\n\nIf the random variables are not independent, they are **dependent**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175