b/ref 17592186426025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426028 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426028 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426028 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186426013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426028 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "660270d9-d112-53f6-9d24-bfc97577f56b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186426020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426029 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b8089fe9-6e61-535d-91d0-a89ef11e11c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426030 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426030 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426030 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426030 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.14$ | $0.16$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.26$ | $0.34$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ce1827c-c98c-5481-b210-2bd31e84c14f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426030 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.14$ | $0.16$ | $0.05$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.26$ | $0.34$ | $0.05$ | $0.65$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.26.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.65\\cdot 0.4=0.26.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.14=0.35\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,2) & =0.16\\ne 0.35\\cdot 0.5={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426031 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426031 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426031 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426031 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(2,3).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.55$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "651363c2-d60f-536f-b27c-718f157b1a2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426031 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(3) \\\\  & =0.45\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0.045.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426032 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426032 #edb/ref 1071 "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $∗$ | $0.27$ | $∗$ |\n| $X=4$ | $∗$ | $0.63$ | $?$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.1$ | $0.9$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(X=4)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7688226a-1fb3-5c5e-91e0-335b33e89079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426032 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,3) & ={f}_{X}(4)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(3) \\\\ P(X=4,Y=3) & =P(X=4)\\cdot P(Y=3) \\\\ 0.63 & =P(X=4)\\cdot 0.9 \\\\ P(X=4) & =\\frac{0.63}{0.9} \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
